            <ArticleTitle>Transcriptional networks in liver and intestinal development.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>a008284</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1101/cshperspect.a008284</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">a008284</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The development of the gastrointestinal tract is a complex process that integrates signaling processes with downstream transcriptional responses. Here, we discuss the regionalization of the primitive gut and formation of the intestine and liver. Anterior-posterior position in the primitive gut is important for establishing regions that will become functional organs. Coordination of signaling between the epithelium and mesenchyme and downstream transcriptional responses is required for intestinal development and homeostasis. Liver development uses a complex transcriptional network that controls the establishment of organ domains, cell differentiation, and adult function. Discussion of these transcriptional mechanisms gives us insight into how the primitive gut, composed of simple endodermal cells, develops into multiple diverse cell types that are organized into complex mature organs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
